# A comment in .gitignore lives ONLY on a line of its own: a `#` at the tail
# of a pattern is part of the pattern to git, not an explanation. Checked here:
# the line `raw/  # 9.7 GB` stops hiding raw/ altogether, and silently.

# secrets, before anything else
.env
*.env
!.env.example

# environments
venv/
tools/venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.uv/

# There is no pytest in .venv and the runner is our own (tests/run.py), but
# the checks are written so pytest would pick them up unchanged -- install it
# and the cache appears.
.pytest_cache/

# Originals: 9.7 GB of scans, 9.4 of them the AnnoPage set from Zenodo
# (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.12788419, CC BY 4.0), re-downloadable by that DOI.
raw/

# 209 MB: the two built books and the paid reading they were made from. The
# reading is not reproducible without another rental; see docs/limits.md.
processed/

# The rent journal: 119 records, $5.527, carrying instance_id and machine_id
# of live vast.ai machines. They have no business going out, and nothing can
# restore them -- keep a copy outside the repository.
runs/

# working files
*.log
out.tgz
.DS_Store
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# ─── benches ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One rule: what goes into git is what cannot be rebuilt for free -- truth, the
# input snapshot, and output paid for on a rented card. Anything the local CPU
# produces from one command does not.

# The bench books themselves: 472 MB annopage, 168 annopage-lite, 100 hard,
# 23 hard36. ONLY TWO OF THE FOUR REBUILD: `books annopage` makes annopage,
# `books subset` makes hard. This once said all four do, which was untrue and
# would have lulled anyone who went looking for the builder.
# `annopage-lite` (squeezed to 1 Mpx) and `hard36` (the 36 hardest pages, long
# side 1800 px, JPEG 92) were built by EXTERNAL scripts that are in neither the
# tree nor the git history -- searched by walking all 4053 objects of the
# repository, not only the reachable ones. The hard36 selection rule is
# recoverable from the truth alone (stable sort of `bench/hard` by pair count,
# take 36; the set converges exactly), but it has an undeclared tie: 21 pages
# have exactly three pairs, 5 were taken, by enumeration order only. So those
# two pdfs exist on one disk and nothing is left to check their manifest
# sha256 against. The pdf sha256 is recorded in manifest.json beside them.
bench/*/*.pdf

# Except the three real scans (9.4 MB): they have no source anywhere -- they
# are the source.
!bench/real/*.pdf

# Sheets with boxes drawn on, for the eye: 2.8 GB for annopage, 162 MB for
# hard36. `books overlay` draws them in minutes and they are looked at once.
bench/*/check/

# Detector output: 25 MB for annopage. `books detect` computes it on the CPU
# for free. The input snapshot is a separate file, detect/run.json (16 KB), and
# it STAYS in git: without it there is no telling which knobs produced the
# numbers in docs/.
bench/*/detect/pages/

# The level-one product, 12 MB per book: `books html` from detect/.
bench/*/html/

# What would go to the VLM: `books feed` writes one picture per text block.
# 12 MB over slovar's 13 pages is 1.4 GB over annopage's 600. For the six
# synthetic books the directory is already closed by the rule above; for
# annopage, annopage-lite, hard and hard36 it was closed by nothing at all
# (`git check-ignore` returned 1).
bench/*/detect/feed/
bench/*/feed/

# The last page of a run as a picture, 1.7 MB -- a debugging trace, not data.
bench/*/dots/_page.png

# An exception to `*.log` above: 72 KB in two files, the only trace of a run
# that was paid for ($0.892 for 600 pages on an RTX 4090; all sixteen rentals
# of that job cost $1.370). Their last lines hold quantities docs/ cites: 8925
# boxes, 23 unparsed, 14.22 s/page. Getting those numbers again costs money.
!bench/*/dots/job.log

# Six synthetic books, from 34 MB (slovar) to 158 (spravochnik). They rebuild
# byte for byte -- the seed, the aging profile and the generator sha256 are in
# manifest.json. The directory is closed by its CONTENTS (`/*`, not `/`): a
# directory closed whole cannot be reopened from inside by any `!`.
bench/spravochnik/*
bench/slovar/*
bench/matematika/*
bench/atlas/*
bench/katalog/*
bench/zhurnal/*

# The manifest is all that is left of a synthetic book: by it one can see that
# a rebuild after an edit to synth.py produced a DIFFERENT book.
!bench/*/manifest.json
